---
import SiteFooter from "../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { handbookOf } from "../../../data/handbooks";
import { url } from "../../../lib/urls";
import "../../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../../styles/article.css";

/**
 * O crédito do modelo de referência, como página.
 *
 * Ela é do handbook da CPU e não genérica porque o que ela tem a dizer é
 * específico: o que foi tomado, o que não foi, e por que a prosa é nossa. Um
 * gerador genérico imprimiria a mesma frase morna para qualquer origem — e o
 * ponto desta página é justamente ser exata sobre uma.
 */
const handbook = handbookOf("cpu")!;
const ref = handbook.reference!;
---

<Base
  domain="cpu"
  title={`Reference model — ${handbook.name}`}
  description="Where the generic microprocessor in this handbook comes from, what we took from it, and what we wrote ourselves."
>
  <SiteNav current="handbooks" />

  <main id="content" class="article">
    <header class="article__head editorial">
      <div>
        <p class="article__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/cpu")}>{handbook.name}</a>
          <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
          <span>reference model</span>
        </p>
        <h1>The machine we did not invent</h1>
        <p class="article__dek">
          The generic microprocessor in this handbook is somebody else&rsquo;s
          model, rebuilt in our engine with his permission. Here is exactly
          what came from him and what did not.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="article__meta mono">
        <p>{ref.author}</p>
        <p>{ref.where}</p>
        <p>
          <a href={url("labs/micro")}>open the lab →</a>
        </p>
      </aside>
    </header>

    <div class="article__body">
      <article class="prose article__prose">
        <blockquote>
          <p>
            The reference model for this machine — its registers, its buses,
            its two instruction formats and its example program — comes from
            <cite>Princípio de Funcionamento de um Microprocessador</cite>, by
            Prof. Filippo Valiante Filho (<a
              href="https://prof.valiante.info"
              rel="noopener">prof.valiante.info</a
            >), used with his permission. The text here is our own.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h2>What we took</h2>
        <p>
          The <strong>shape of the machine</strong>, and the order in which it
          is worth meeting. An 8-bit accumulator processor with a program
          counter, an instruction register, an address latch and a data latch,
          an accumulator and a temporary, an H/L pair holding a 16-bit address
          in two 8-bit halves, a stack pointer that is declared and wired to
          nothing, and a status register. One memory, with the program at
          <code>0000</code> and the data at <code>2000</code>. One 16-line
          address bus and one 8-line data bus.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>two instruction formats</strong>: opcode plus value in
          two bytes, opcode plus high byte plus low byte in three. The three
          instructions <code>LOAD</code>, <code>ADD</code> and
          <code>STORE</code>, with the opcodes <code>86</code>,
          <code>8B</code> and <code>B7</code> — deliberately invented numbers,
          because the machine is generic in the literal sense. The example
          program that adds <code>0A</code>, <code>05</code> and
          <code>12</code> and stores the result at <code>2000</code>. The list
          of cycle phases, and the timing table that shows one program as one
          page of instants.
        </p>

        <h2>What we added</h2>
        <p>
          Three instructions the deck does not have —
          <code>LOADM</code>, <code>JMP</code> and <code>JZ</code> — because
          with three instructions you cannot write a loop, and a machine that
          cannot loop is a machine you can only watch. Their opcodes are as
          arbitrary as the originals, and picked only so as not to collide.
        </p>
        <p>
          And depth. The deck draws the ALU as two boxes and stops, because a
          slide does not open; here the same ALU opens into an adder, into one
          bit of it, into the XOR, into the NAND, into the transistors and the
          supply rail — nine levels from the system down. That is the part of
          the model that only exists because it runs.
        </p>

        <h2>What is ours, and why it matters</h2>
        <p>
          Every word of prose on this site. Permission to use a model is not
          permission to reuse a lecturer&rsquo;s sentences: transcribing his
          text would be plagiarism with a licence attached. So the articles
          were written from scratch, and every technical claim in them stands
          on its own — traced to a primary source, or to the model in the lab,
          which you can run and disagree with.
        </p>
        <p>
          The one place where his document is quoted as a document is the
          timing table, which we hold as an
          <strong>oracle</strong>: our machine is run against his table cell by
          cell, eleven rows, in a test that fails if we drift. Using someone
          else&rsquo;s table that way is the strongest form of credit we know —
          it means his page, and not our opinion, decides when we are wrong.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href={url("labs/micro")}>The lab</a> ·
          <a href={url("handbooks/cpu/articles/the-instruction-cycle")}
            >The instruction cycle</a
          > ·
          <a href={url("handbooks/cpu")}>Back to the handbook</a>
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
